window.__finlexDocLoaded(10214,{"id":10214,"articles":[{"id":1000887,"no":"Overview","heading":"Scope & Core Objective (Public Summary)","text":"Jointly developed with the IASB (converged with IFRS 15), establishing the five-step revenue recognition model based on transfer of control of promised goods or services to customers.","seq":0},{"id":1000888,"no":"ASC 606-10-25 / 32","heading":"Five-Step Model, Licensing IP & Key US GAAP Elections","text":"• Five-Step Core Principle (ASC 606-10-05-4): (1) Identify contract (collectibility = 'probable', ~75%+); (2) Identify distinct performance obligations (shipping/handling may be elected as a fulfillment activity); (3) Determine transaction price (variable consideration constraint, significant financing component, non-cash consideration measured at contract inception); (4) Allocate transaction price via SSP; (5) Recognize revenue over time or at a point in time.\n• Licensing Intellectual Property (ASC 606-10-55-58): Distinguishes 'Functional IP' (point in time, e.g., software, films) vs. 'Symbolic IP' (over time, e.g., brands, logos).\n• Sales Tax Election (ASC 606-10-32-2A): Policy election to exclude all sales/similar taxes from the transaction price.","seq":1},{"id":1000889,"no":"US GAAP vs. IFRS / CAS","heading":"Key Cross-Framework Differences","text":"• Subtle Divergences from IFRS 15 / CAS 14: (1) Step 1 Collectibility — ASC 606 'probable' (~75%+) is higher than IFRS 15 (>50%); (2) Shipping & Handling — ASC 606 permits an explicit fulfillment-cost election after control transfer (not in IFRS 15); (3) Non-cash consideration — ASC 606 mandates measurement at contract inception (IFRS 15 does not specify date); (4) IP licensing uses Functional vs. Symbolic distinction.","seq":2},{"id":1000890,"no":"Official Access","heading":"Official FASB Codification & Reference Entry","text":"Official Portal (FASB Accounting Standards Codification®): https://asc.fasb.org/\nStatus: in_force (FASB ASC free Basic View available at https://asc.fasb.org/).","seq":3}]});
